From: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: fix stale flow-index delete
 clobbering a live entry

ppe_offload_flow_replace() programs every flow with
PPE_FLOW_OP_MODE_HASH, under which hardware places (or, per
ppe_flow_entry_add()'s own doc comment, *overwrites*) an entry purely
by hashing its match key -- independent of insertion order. The same
5-tuple always lands on the same hardware index. ppe_offload_flow_destroy()
then deletes by a flow_index captured once, at add time, into the
per-cookie struct ppe_offload_flow_entry, with nothing checking that
the hardware slot at that index still belongs to this cookie by the
time the delete actually runs.

nf_flow_table_offload.c dispatches ADD and DEL work items to two
separate workqueues (nf_flow_offload_add_wq / nf_flow_offload_del_wq)
with no ordering guarantee between them. That makes the following
sequence possible whenever a connection's teardown is still in flight
when an identical-tuple reconnect shows up (e.g. an old, expiring
keep-alive to some destination:port, superseded by a fresh connection
to the exact same destination:port from the exact same LAN-side
address:port):

  1. Flow A (5-tuple T) is added, hardware assigns index N. Our
     driver's cookie_A entry stores flow_index=N.
  2. A's FLOW_CLS_DESTROY is queued but hasn't run yet.
  3. Flow B, with the *same* 5-tuple T (a same-tuple reconnect), is
     added before A's destroy runs. Hashing T again yields the same
     index N, so hardware silently overwrites A's hardware entry with
     B's. Our driver's cookie_B entry also stores flow_index=N.
  4. A's delayed destroy finally runs, looks up cookie_A (still
     present -- nothing removed it), and unconditionally deletes
     index N -- which now holds B's live, active hardware entry, not
     A's.

The result is a currently-healthy, unrelated-in-software-terms
connection (B) silently losing its hardware flow-table entry, with no
error visible anywhere: conntrack, ping, DNS and every other flow stay
completely unaffected, matching this driver's observed live-testing
symptom of one specific connection breaking under real traffic while
everything else stays healthy.

Track, in priv->flow_owner[], which struct ppe_offload_flow_entry
currently owns each hardware flow-table index. When an add lands on an
index another tracked entry still claims, mark that other entry
superseded (flow_valid = false) instead of leaving it pointing at a
slot it no longer owns. A superseded entry's later destroy then skips
ppe_flow_entry_del() entirely -- by the time it runs, that index names
a different, live flow's hardware entry, not this one's -- while still
correctly dereffing its own host/nexthop/pub-IP pool entries, which are
separate, independently refcounted resources unaffected by the flow-
table collision.

This does not resolve the separate, still-open question (see
project_flint3_ppe_hw_offload_research.md) of why some earlier clean
tests observed zero flows being offered to hardware in the outbound
direction at all -- if that observation holds in general, this bug
requires flows to reach the add path to begin with, which needs
reconciling with it directly rather than assumed.

flow_owner[]'s clear in ppe_offload_flow_destroy() is keyed on pointer
identity (flow_owner[index] == entry), not on this entry's own
flow_valid flag: if this entry's own add later failed (kzalloc or
rhashtable_insert_fast) after it had already superseded some earlier
owner, flow_owner[] can still point at *this* entry even though its
own flow_valid was never set true, and gating the clear on flow_valid
instead of identity would leave that pointer dangling once this entry
is kfree()'d -- a real, if OOM-only, use-after-free. The identity check
makes "flow_owner never points at a freed entry" hold unconditionally.

Also logs the supersede event itself (pr_info_ratelimited, matching
this file's existing temporary instrumentation) -- without it, whether
this path is ever actually exercised on live hardware can only be
inferred from duplicate flow_index values surviving ratelimiting in
the existing accept log, which is easy to miss.

Compile-tested (clean module build, drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe
rebuilt standalone against the existing kernel build tree). Not yet
tested against live hardware or under real reconnect-churn traffic --
this needs a targeted test that forces a same-5-tuple reconnect while
the original connection's teardown is still in flight, not just
generic throughput load.

Signed-off-by: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_offload.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_offload.c
@@ -63,6 +63,7 @@
  * hardware-testing-time question; nothing here changes it.
  */
 
+#include <linux/bitfield.h>
 #include <linux/errno.h>
 #include <linux/etherdevice.h>
 #include <linux/if_ether.h>
@@ -89,7 +90,25 @@
 #include "ppe_nexthop_pool.h"
 #include "ppe_offload.h"
 #include "ppe_pub_ip_pool.h"
+#include "ppe_regs.h"
 
+/* Total number of hardware-addressable flow-table slots, derived from
+ * the width of the index field hardware reports a HASH-mode add's
+ * result in -- used to size priv->flow_owner (see struct
+ * ppe_offload_priv) below.
+ */
+#define PPE_OFFLOAD_FLOW_TBL_ENTRIES (FIELD_MAX(PPE_IN_FLOW_TBL_OP_ENTRY_INDEX) + 1)
+
+/* @flow_valid: Whether @flow_index still names a hardware flow-table
+ * slot this struct owns. PPE_FLOW_OP_MODE_HASH places (or overwrites)
+ * an entry purely by hashing its match key, independent of insertion
+ * order, so a same-5-tuple reconnect can land a new flow on the exact
+ * slot an older, not-yet-destroyed flow still thinks it owns. When
+ * that happens the add path clears this on the superseded entry (see
+ * struct ppe_offload_priv::flow_owner), and its later destroy must
+ * then skip deleting @flow_index, since by then it names a different,
+ * live flow's hardware entry, not this one's.
+ */
 struct ppe_offload_flow_entry {
 	struct rhash_head node;
 	unsigned long cookie;
@@ -97,6 +116,7 @@
 	u32 host_index;
 	u32 nexthop_index;
 	u32 pub_ip_index;
+	bool flow_valid;
 };
 
 static const struct rhashtable_params ppe_offload_flow_ht_params = {
@@ -113,6 +133,13 @@
  * @nexthop_pool: Refcounted nexthop table allocator.
  * @pub_ip_pool: Refcounted public IP address table allocator.
  * @flow_table: Offloaded flows, keyed by TC flow cookie.
+ * @flow_owner: One entry per hardware flow-table index, naming which
+ * (if any) &struct ppe_offload_flow_entry currently owns it in
+ * hardware. Only needed because of PPE_FLOW_OP_MODE_HASH's
+ * add-or-overwrite semantics -- see ppe_offload_flow_entry::flow_valid
+ * and ppe_offload_flow_replace()/_destroy() for how it's used to stop
+ * a superseded entry's later destroy from deleting a different, live
+ * flow's hardware entry.
  * @lock: Serializes the whole replace/destroy sequence -- looking up
  * @flow_table, allocating from the pools, and programming hardware --
  * against concurrent calls for different flows, mirroring
@@ -124,6 +151,7 @@
 	struct ppe_nexthop_pool nexthop_pool;
 	struct ppe_pub_ip_pool pub_ip_pool;
 	struct rhashtable flow_table;
+	struct ppe_offload_flow_entry **flow_owner;
 	struct mutex lock;
 };
 
@@ -443,6 +471,20 @@
 	if (ret)
 		goto deref_host;
 
+	/* HASH mode places this entry purely by hashing its match key, so
+	 * an identical key (e.g. a same-5-tuple reconnect) always lands on
+	 * the same index regardless of which flow got there first -- if
+	 * another tracked flow already owned this index, hardware has just
+	 * silently overwritten its entry with ours. Mark it superseded so
+	 * its own destroy, whenever it runs, doesn't delete what is now
+	 * our hardware entry.
+	 */
+	if (priv->flow_owner[flow_index]) {
+		pr_info_ratelimited("ppe_offload: superseded cookie=%lu at flow_index=%u (new cookie=%lu)\n",
+				     priv->flow_owner[flow_index]->cookie, flow_index, cls->cookie);
+		priv->flow_owner[flow_index]->flow_valid = false;
+	}
+
 	entry = kzalloc(sizeof(*entry), GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (!entry) {
 		ret = -ENOMEM;
@@ -454,11 +496,14 @@
 	entry->host_index = host_index;
 	entry->nexthop_index = nexthop_index;
 	entry->pub_ip_index = pub_ip_index;
+	entry->flow_valid = true;
 
 	ret = rhashtable_insert_fast(&priv->flow_table, &entry->node, ppe_offload_flow_ht_params);
 	if (ret)
 		goto free_entry;
 
+	priv->flow_owner[flow_index] = entry;
+
 	mutex_unlock(&priv->lock);
 
 	pr_info_ratelimited("ppe_offload: accepted flow_index=%u host_index=%u nexthop_index=%u pub_ip_index=%u src=%pI4->%pI4 odev=%s\n",
@@ -497,7 +542,24 @@
 
 	rhashtable_remove_fast(&priv->flow_table, &entry->node, ppe_offload_flow_ht_params);
 
-	ppe_flow_entry_del(priv->ppe_dev, entry->flow_index);
+	/* Clear flow_owner[] by pointer identity, unconditionally on
+	 * flow_valid: if this entry's own add later failed after already
+	 * superseding some earlier owner (see ppe_offload_flow_replace()),
+	 * flow_owner[] can still point at *this* entry even though
+	 * flow_valid was never set true, and leaving it there would dangle
+	 * once this entry is kfree()'d below. Gating the clear on
+	 * flow_valid instead of identity would miss exactly that case.
+	 *
+	 * If a later add for a different flow already overwrote this
+	 * entry's hardware slot, flow_owner[] now points at that other,
+	 * still-live entry instead -- the identity check leaves it alone,
+	 * and flow_valid being false skips deleting hardware out from
+	 * under it.
+	 */
+	if (priv->flow_owner[entry->flow_index] == entry)
+		priv->flow_owner[entry->flow_index] = NULL;
+	if (entry->flow_valid)
+		ppe_flow_entry_del(priv->ppe_dev, entry->flow_index);
 	ppe_host_pool_deref(priv->ppe_dev, &priv->host_pool, entry->host_index);
 	ppe_nexthop_pool_deref(priv->ppe_dev, &priv->nexthop_pool, entry->nexthop_index);
 	ppe_pub_ip_pool_deref(priv->ppe_dev, &priv->pub_ip_pool, entry->pub_ip_index);
@@ -769,6 +831,11 @@
 	ppe_pub_ip_pool_init(&priv->pub_ip_pool);
 	mutex_init(&priv->lock);
 
+	priv->flow_owner = devm_kcalloc(ppe_dev->dev, PPE_OFFLOAD_FLOW_TBL_ENTRIES,
+					 sizeof(*priv->flow_owner), GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!priv->flow_owner)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
 	ret = rhashtable_init(&priv->flow_table, &ppe_offload_flow_ht_params);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
